## Proof

We compute $\Omega_2^{\mathrm{SO}}(\{\mathrm{pt}\})$, the oriented bordism group of a point in dimension 2. By definition, this is the group of closed oriented 2-manifolds modulo oriented bordism (maps to a point carry no additional data), with addition given by disjoint union.

**Claim:** $\Omega_2^{\mathrm{SO}}(\{\mathrm{pt}\}) = 0$.

**Proof that every closed oriented 2-manifold is an oriented boundary.**

Let $M$ be a closed oriented 2-manifold. By the classification theorem for compact surfaces, $M$ is diffeomorphic to a disjoint union
$$M \cong \coprod_{i=1}^{k} \Sigma_{g_i}$$
where $\Sigma_g$ denotes the closed oriented surface of genus $g$ (with $\Sigma_0 = S^2$). It suffices to show that each connected component $\Sigma_g$ is null-bordant, since a disjoint union of boundaries is a boundary.

For each $g \geq 0$, consider the **handlebody** $H_g$: the orientable 3-manifold obtained by attaching $g$ 1-handles to a 3-ball $B^3$. Concretely, $H_g$ is a regular neighborhood of a wedge of $g$ circles embedded in $\mathbb{R}^3$. The handlebody $H_g$ is a compact orientable 3-manifold whose boundary is
$$\partial H_g \cong \Sigma_g.$$

The orientation on $H_g$ (inherited from $\mathbb{R}^3$) induces, via the outward-normal-first convention, the standard orientation on $\partial H_g \cong \Sigma_g$. Hence $\Sigma_g$, with its standard orientation, is the oriented boundary of the oriented 3-manifold $H_g$:
$$\Sigma_g = \partial H_g \quad \text{in } \Omega_2^{\mathrm{SO}}.$$

Therefore
$$M = \coprod_{i=1}^{k} \Sigma_{g_i} = \partial\!\left(\coprod_{i=1}^{k} H_{g_i}\right),$$
so $M$ is null-bordant. Since every closed oriented 2-manifold represents the zero class in $\Omega_2^{\mathrm{SO}}(\{\mathrm{pt}\})$, we conclude
$$\boxed{\Omega_2^{\mathrm{SO}}(\{\mathrm{pt}\}) = 0.}$$

**Consistency check.** This agrees with the general structure of the oriented bordism ring: $\Omega_*^{\mathrm{SO}} \cong \mathbb{Z}[\mathbb{CP}^2, \mathbb{CP}^4, \dots]$ is a polynomial ring over $\mathbb{Z}$ with one generator in each dimension $4k$, so $\Omega_0^{\mathrm{SO}} \cong \mathbb{Z}$, $\Omega_1^{\mathrm{SO}} = \Omega_2^{\mathrm{SO}} = \Omega_3^{\mathrm{SO}} = 0$, and $\Omega_4^{\mathrm{SO}} \cong \mathbb{Z}$, consistent with our direct geometric argument.

